NAME
    clipboard - read and write the system clipboard

SYNOPSIS
    clipboard
    clipboard paste
    clipboard copy [TEXT...]
    clipboard clear

DESCRIPTION
    Reads and writes the system clipboard: the one piece of text that
    every program on the machine shares. A windowed program's Ctrl+C
    puts its selection there, Ctrl+V reads it back, and this command
    does the same from a shell or a script.

    With no argument, or with paste, it prints the clipboard to
    standard output. With copy it replaces the clipboard: the TEXT
    arguments are joined with spaces and a newline is appended, so
    `clipboard copy hello world` copies one line; with no TEXT it reads
    standard input instead, so `command | clipboard copy` and
    `clipboard copy < file` both work. With clear it empties the
    clipboard.

    The clipboard is the /dev/clipboard character device. Writing the
    device replaces what was there; reading it walks the current text
    from the beginning to the end. It lives in RAM and does not survive
    a reboot.

FILES
    /dev/clipboard   the clipboard itself

EXIT STATUS
    0 on success, 1 on an unknown command or when the device cannot be
    opened.

SEE ALSO
    cat(1), vedit(1), sticky(1)
